  <h2 style="margin-top: 0;">3. The energy neutral neighbourhood</h2>
  <p>Net reinforcement is not the only solution</p>
  <p>Jan Pellis is a strategist at grid operator Stedin and is responsible for the pilot High Dalem 2.0. Grid operators play a central role in the changing energy landscape. How does he think the role of Stedin will change? What are the problems he comes across? What should be done differently?</p>
  <p>"As Stedin, we are responsible for gas and electricity grids, down to the meter box. That means we look at the future, the energy transition. Because the Netherlands is going to make a massive switch to all-electric, we must reinforce the current grids. But developments are going so fast that we need to look for other solutions. For example, leaving off peaks in the grid and not having the demand from the grid occur at all, once but spread out. We must use smart solutions such as storing energy from electric cars and storing energy locally. These elements were part of Hoog Dalem 1.0. (See chapter 2)"</p>
  <p>Net reinforcement?</p>
  <p>We take it one step further in Hoog Dalem 2.0. Residents can share supply and demand of electricity through a local market on block chain technology. As a grid operator, we can use this local market to utilize flexibility in the supply and demand of the users, reducing the need for reinforcement of the grid. This could, for example, be a good solution for areas that are moving away from natural gas and will use and produce much more electricity as a result of the heat pumps and solar panels. Maybe a definitive solution, but at least until a grid reinforcement has been realised, and this will not be a superfluous luxury given the thousands of neighbourhoods that will become more sustainable in the coming years.</p>
  <p>Local energy markets</p>
  <p>Hoog Dalem 2.0 is a good example of a sustainable, all-electric neighbourhood. The pilot in line with the increasing trend of local initiatives and energy cooperatives. Our challenge is how we can</p>
  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-top: 30px;">
    <div style="width: 45%;">
      <p>facilitate it, coordinate it with each other and keep the energy in the neighbourhood without being disconnected from the net. Because if the wind blows hard on the North Sea, the neighbourhood can provide an extra storage or storage by taking energy from the grid. Hoog Dalem 2.0 offers a locally attractive energy picture and, moreover, the entire system can benefit.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 45%;">
      <p>Blueprint</p>
      <p>As Stedin, we do this together with partners and without aiming to become the owner of the local energy market. We are a grid operator, not a market operator. We want to share with the outside world. This is not just about the grid, but about the energy infrastructure, we were 100% focused on the grid, but now we are focused on the energy infrastructure, we have to offer more. Of course, we remain responsible for the quality of the network, but we now also want to work with partners and customers about digitalisation, the development of platforms, the availability of data and the distribution of supply and demand in the grid.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
