  <p>Sustainable with social components</p>
  <p>Spencer Schools is responsible within the Bo-Ex housing corporation for making thousands of social rental homes energy neutral. How does it intend to do this big job? What are the problems it comes across? What tips does it have for other housing associations that want to follow?</p>
  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-top: 30px;">
    <div style="width: 45%;">
      <p>Schools: "Primarily, Bo-Ex is going to help people who have trouble getting a house to get an affordable home. We do this in a sustainable manner and this automatically leads to lower CO2 emissions. This is in line with our creed: good things come to those who make them happen. We use the energy neutral construction of homes and we are also looking at the energy transition. It starts with lowering CO2 emissions."</p>
      <p>Energy positivity</p>
      <p>For Bo-Ex, looking strongly at where renewable energy comes from and how we can keep it affordable. This can involve integral solutions, such as solar panels, and we are also looking at determining aspects. For the sustainability of the homes, we are looking at the energy mix. We look broader and add the concept of energy positivity. In other words, we want to add something positive, a social component, in the form of an energy surplus that we can redistribute.</p>
